  "selected": [
    {
      "id": "anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "methods/anomaly-baselines-and-climate-indices.md",
      "sha256": "e0afb5c187aca1e075f9549153b1e896320bb0539544f66d3293fab4b4b77c92",
      "reason": "The whole assessment is built on Nino 3.4 anomalies defined against a 1950-2014 monthly climatology, so its rules on anomaly baselines, on not mixing seasons, and on judging events on anomalies with persistence directly shape the index and the seasonal-cycle/exceedance numbers.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Compute each source's monthly Niño 3.4 index (5S–5N, 170W–120W) as its anomaly from its own 1950–2014 calendar-month climatology—each month minus that month's multi-year mean—not an observed/WOA climatology; state the climatology and baseline, since a different baseline adds a monthly offset.",
        "Count months with |anomaly| > 1.0 directly; the question asks for months, not events, so do not apply event persistence (`min_duration`) and do not mix DJF/MAM/JJA/SON with WOA calendar quarters—the seasonal amplitude requested is per calendar month."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "omip-weighted-means-and-integrals",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "methods/omip-weighted-means-and-integrals.md",
      "sha256": "89de52676ae54db8f5897bc77a08c78de3f776696db0020935a38b790c302cbe",
      "reason": "The Nino 3.4 index is a surface regional mean over 5S-5N, 170W-120W, and this fragment states that surface means are area-weighted over the top level, which fixes how each source's box average must be computed.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Compute the regional mean with area weights and valid cells only: Σ `tos`·`areacello` / Σ `areacello` over the Niño 3.4 box; unweighted `mean()` over (y,x) is wrong, as is an unweighted latitude mean on a regular grid.",
        "Restrict numerator and denominator to the same valid ocean cells (NaN-aware weights); do not count land/missing cells in the area."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-static-grid-fields",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-static-grid-fields.md",
      "sha256": "2ad0548ca0993fa57d8f1fad27a267dcf9c0e1d007a74343d4bbdbf5b4f1ca17",
      "reason": "The question supplies each model on its own native grid with its own published cell areas, and this fragment defines areacello/volcello as the tracer-cell area and volume used for such weighting.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Use each model's published `areacello` (tracer-cell area, zero or missing over land) as the horizontal weight; on curvilinear grids it is the only correct area weight. Total ocean area differs slightly between models, so state the area used rather than rescaling."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-grid-labels",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "datasets/cmip6-grid-labels.md",
      "sha256": "e349f9e305621b6c10b35cccd0490da521cc7e3ba08bfb53890479f81024d117",
      "reason": "CESM2 and MPI-ESM1-2-LR are gn while GFDL-ESM4 is gr (already regridded to 1°x1°), which changes the masks and which operations on each native grid are exact when building the box index.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "The question says “each on its own native grid”, but GFDL-ESM4's local record is `grid_label='gr'`, remapped to a 180×360 1° archive grid; use its published `areacello` on that grid and state this difference."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-tos-bulk-top-cell",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "concepts/cmip6-tos-bulk-top-cell.md",
      "sha256": "4acdeba0f49784d4435dbc4eed34ca9777254313daa951641759fa02df24cf64",
      "reason": "The models' tos is top-cell bulk potential temperature, not a skin temperature, which constrains how the modelled Nino 3.4 amplitude may be compared with the ERSSTv5-based one.",
      "status": "not_applicable",
      "not_applicable_reason": "the question supplies `tos` directly; the top-cell/under-ice definition does not alter a tropical Niño 3.4 index, and no `thetao` substitution is needed."
    },
    {
      "id": "cmip6-experiments-historical-ssp245",
      "severity": "should",
      "path": "datasets/cmip6-experiments-historical-ssp245.md",
      "sha256": "be5515ae7b8c8826c4e329bddcd5cbadc8e3e000292b7f6e83e1537af36a990c",
      "reason": "historical is a coupled run with free internal variability whose years are not synchronised with observed years, so the model-minus-ERSSTv5 amplitude bias and the year-by-year exceedance counts must be stated as a statistical, not event-matched, comparison.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "Historical runs are coupled and have internal variability: a given model year (e.g. 1997) is not expected to match the observed year. Compare amplitude statistics over the full 1950–2014 window, not year-by-year or event timing."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "monthly-means-are-time-averages",
      "severity": "must",
      "path": "pitfalls/monthly-means-are-time-averages.md",
      "sha256": "df6b5c23460f88f1522e5c6a665e1efbb8322c3ae876c885530f3019aa284ee0",
      "reason": "The index, its all-month and per-calendar-month standard deviations and the exceedance counts are all computed from monthly means, i.e. from averages over all time steps, which affects how the amplitude and phase-locking results should be read.",
      "status": "condensed",
      "notes": [
        "The monthly `tos` values are time averages; standard deviations and threshold counts of monthly Niño 3.4 anomalies describe monthly variability only and cannot be read as sub-monthly variance or daily extremes.",
        "For the monthly climatology, average each calendar month's values across years with a plain mean; day weights do not enter a monthly climatology (`compute_anomaly_vs_own_climatology`/index tools do this)."
      ]
    },
